company.name=Testing Environment
```

---

## application-prod.properties

```properties
server.port=9090

company.name=Production Environment
```

Each environment has its own settings.

---

# Activating a Profile

Inside `application.properties`

```properties
spring.profiles.active=dev
```

Spring loads

```text
application-dev.properties
```

If you write

```properties
spring.profiles.active=prod
```

Spring loads

```text
application-prod.properties
```

---

# Real Project Example

Suppose you have

```properties
# application-dev.properties

spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/employeedb
```

and

```properties
# application-prod.properties

spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://192.168.1.100:3306/employeedb
```

Your Java code never changes.

```java
@Service
public class EmployeeService {

}
```

Only the active profile changes.

---

# @Profile Annotation

Profiles are not only for properties.

You can also create beans based on the active profile.

Example:

```java
public interface NotificationService {

    void send();

}
```

---

Development implementation

```java
@Service
@Profile("dev")
public class ConsoleNotificationService
        implements NotificationService {

    @Override
    public void send() {
        System.out.println("Console Notification");
    }

}
```

---

Production implementation

```java
@Service
@Profile("prod")
public class EmailNotificationService
        implements NotificationService {

    @Override
    public void send() {
        System.out.println("Email Notification");
    }

}
```

---

Suppose

```properties
spring.profiles.active=dev
```

Spring creates

```text
ConsoleNotificationService
```

If

```properties
spring.profiles.active=prod
```

Spring creates

```text
EmailNotificationService
```

No code changes.

